From c4acaa2bfcaab5e8467a937c1fef2ffc79503130 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ae86zhizhi <550149470@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 14:47:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(pcre2): align Unicode class semantics with the
 reference engine

PCRE2 denotes different sets than `regex`/`fancy-regex` for two classes
that appear in pre-tokenization patterns, so a Split compiled for PCRE2
produced different pretokens than both the reference tokenizer and this
crate's own scanner.

`\s`: under PCRE2_UCP this compiles to PT_SPACE, evaluated as
HSPACE u VSPACE u general category Z rather than the White_Space property.
PCRE2's HSPACE_LIST still contains U+180E MONGOLIAN VOWEL SEPARATOR, which
was Zs/White_Space through Unicode 6.2 and became Cf in 6.3; PCRE2 keeps it
for \h compatibility, so this is a definition difference, not a stale
table (still present in 10.46). Effect: " " + U+180E + a letter lands on a
different pretoken boundary and silently changes token ids.

`\p{<script>}`: PCRE2 reads a bare script name as Script_Extensions while
`regex` reads it as Script. For Han that is 308 codepoints, including CJK
punctuation in everyday use.

Both are fixed by spelling the class out when compiling for PCRE2. Patterns
using constructs that cannot be rewritten faithfully (\S inside a class,
POSIX [:space:]) now fall back to the non-PCRE2 engine, which already
agrees with the reference -- a performance fallback, not a correctness one.

Tests sweep every scalar value and assert the rewritten pattern denotes
exactly the reference set.
